digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56698-w
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Develop and evaluate a standardised faecal small-RNA sequencing framework to identify a reproducible core faecal miRNA profile in healthy mice, assess its longitudinal stability, and test whether chronic Trichuris muris infection produces biologically informative miRNA deviations linked to intestinal barrier disruption, wound healing and fibrosis.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) The retained and repaired terminal conclusions jointly motivate the paper-level research proposal."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) Mature miRNAs are accessible in non-invasive faecal and other body-fluid samples, and extracellular-vesicle encapsulation provides a plausible mechanism for enhanced extracellular miRNA stability."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) Because Trichuris muris is a mouse model of the human whipworm Trichuris trichiura and both systems are closely related intestinal helminth infections with substantial health relevance, the T. muris model is a relevant context for studying intestinal responses to whipworm infection."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Prior disease-associated faecal-miRNA findings, recent high-throughput faecal sRNA-seq profiling, and the stability, detectability and trait-dependent variability of faecal miRNAs motivate systematic, standardised faecal sRNA-seq for robust faecal-miRNA characterisation."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Given an optimised pipeline and core faecal miRNA profile, and the need to show longitudinal stability before treating a health profile as a reliable baseline, longitudinal faecal-miRNA analysis in healthy mice is an appropriate next evaluation."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) Because chronic Trichuris muris infection is expected to alter faecal miRNA profiles and an optimised faecal sRNA-seq pipeline is available, applying the pipeline to infected mice is an appropriate hypothesis-generating analysis of infection-associated faecal-miRNA changes."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) Differential expression of miRNAs associated with wound healing, barrier integrity and fibrosis during chronic Trichuris muris infection supports the hypothesis that miRNA-mediated pathways may be involved in tissue damage repair during chronic infection."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) Together, the optimised faecal sRNA-seq pipeline, healthy-mouse core and longitudinal baseline evaluation, chronic Trichuris muris infection analysis, and repair-associated miRNA changes support the paper’s aim to test whether faecal miRNA profiling can define a reproducible health-associated core signature and reveal biologically relevant deviations during chronic infection."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Because faecal miRNAs can be accessible and stabilised extracellularly, and intestinal miRNAs regulate barrier, metabolic, immune and microbial homeostasis, faecal miRNAs are reasonable candidates to evaluate as potential indicators of intestinal physiology and pathology."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) The detection of intestinal epithelial cell-derived miRNAs in faecal extracellular vesicles supports profiling these faecal miRNAs as a promising, non-invasive approach for studying intestinal disease, while leaving biomarker informativeness to be tested."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Since Trichuris muris perturbs intestinal epithelial, microbiota and mucosal immune processes that are regulated by intestinal miRNAs, this infection motivates testing whether faecal miRNA profiles change in relation to those intestinal processes."];
  "n10" [label="(8,0,0) Currently in gastrointestinal cancers and inflammatory bowel disease, disease-associated alterations in specific faecal miRNAs have been identified as novel diagnostic tools and indicators of disease severity and prognosis."];
  "n12" [label="(24,0,0) Currently faecal miRNAs are stable, detectable and of potential clinical interest as non-invasive diagnostic and prognostic biomarkers, but their profiles vary with traits and lifestyle."];
  "n14" [label="(9,0,0) Currently there is substantial heterogeneity in study design for faecal miRNA work (storage, isolation, analysis), which limits reproducibility and practical utility of findings."];
  "n15" [label="(11,0,0) Currently it remains unclear how reproducible faecal miRNA profiles are across studies and whether they are stable in health and homeostasis."];
  "n17" [label="(12,0,0) Currently the authors developed an optimised faecal small RNA sequencing pipeline and performed a meta-analysis of recent murine faecal sRNA-seq datasets to address these limitations."];
  "n2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently mature miRNAs can be detected extracellularly in many body fluids, including faeces, so they are accessible in non-invasive samples."];
  "n20" [label="(0,0,0) Currently to claim that a faecal miRNA profile is a reliable baseline of health, one must show that this profile is longitudinally stable in healthy animals under homeostatic conditions."];
  "n22" [label="(16,0,0) Currently Trichuris muris is a mouse model of the human whipworm Trichuris trichiura, which infects over 500 million people and causes chronic diarrhoeal disease in children."];
  "n23" [label="(17,0,0) Currently during Trichuris muris pathogenesis the parasite disrupts the intestinal epithelium, perturbs the microbiota, and induces Th1- or Th2-polarised mucosal immune responses, all of which are processes known to be regulated by intestinal miRNAs."];
  "n28" [label="(20,0,0) Currently chronic Trichuris muris infection induces differential expression of miRNAs associated with wound healing, barrier integrity and fibrosis."];
  "n3" [label="(3,0,0) Currently encapsulation of miRNAs within the phospholipid membranes of extracellular vesicles (EVs) enhances their extracellular stability."];
  "n33" [label="(0,0,0) If intestinal epithelial cell-derived miRNAs are present in faecal extracellular vesicles and faecal miRNAs can serve as informative biomarkers of intestinal state, then profiling these faecal miRNAs constitutes a promising non-invasive tool to study intestinal disease."];
  "n37" [label="(0,0,0) If study methods are heterogeneous and cross-study as well as longitudinal stability are unknown, then existing findings cannot yet support robust, generalisable faecal miRNA biomarkers or mechanistic conclusions, so standardised approaches are required."];
  "n38" [label="(0,0,0) Conclusion: Given substantial methodological heterogeneity and limited understanding of faecal miRNA reproducibility and stability, therefore a standardised, optimised faecal sRNA-seq pipeline and cross-study meta-analysis are needed to identify robust core faecal miRNA profiles."];
  "n39" [label="(0,0,0) If one applies a standardised, optimised sequencing pipeline and integrates multiple compatible datasets via meta-analysis, then one can assess reproducibility and identify a robust core faecal miRNA profile across laboratories while establishing best-practice guidelines."];
  "n40" [label="(0,0,0) Conclusion: Given that the authors developed an optimised faecal sRNA-seq pipeline and performed a meta-analysis in line with methodological recommendations, therefore they can define best practices and a core faecal miRNA profile consistent across laboratory practices."];
  "n41" [label="(0,0,0) If a core faecal miRNA profile and best-practice pipeline are available and a reliable health baseline requires demonstration of longitudinal stability, then a longitudinal study of faecal miRNAs in healthy mice using this pipeline is required."];
  "n44" [label="(0,0,0) Conclusion: Given that Trichuris muris infection disrupts intestinal epithelium, perturbs the microbiota and alters Th1/Th2-polarised mucosal immune responses, all of which are miRNA-regulated processes, therefore chronic Trichuris muris infection is expected to induce characteristic changes in faecal miRNA profiles associated with tissue damage and repair."];
  "n45" [label="(0,0,0) If a specific infection is predicted to alter faecal miRNA profiles and a robust optimised faecal sRNA-seq pipeline is available, then applying this pipeline to infected animals can both characterise infection-induced miRNA changes and support hypothesis generation about disease mechanisms."];
  "n47" [label="(0,0,0) Currently Trichuris muris infection in mice and Trichuris trichiura infection in humans represent closely related helminth systems with substantial global health impact and shared intestinal localisation."];
  "n49" [label="(0,0,0) If tissue repair-associated miRNAs change during a chronic Th1-polarised infection that lacks typical Th2 wound-healing cytokines, then miRNA-mediated pathways may provide an alternative route for tissue damage repair in that context."];
  "n5" [label="(5,0,0) Currently in the mammalian intestine, miRNAs are essential regulators of barrier integrity, metabolism, mucosal immune responses and microbial homeostasis."];
  "n51" [label="(0,0,0) If an optimised, standardised faecal sRNA-seq pipeline can define a reproducible and longitudinally stable core faecal miRNA profile in health and reveal infection-induced deviations linked to tissue repair and barrier regulation, then such a pipeline should be developed and applied as a non-invasive framework for studying intestinal health and disease mechanisms."];
  "n7" [label="(6,0,0) Currently miRNAs produced by intestinal epithelial cells are detectable in extracellular vesicles in faeces and are proposed as a promising non-invasive tool to study intestinal disease."];
